// A function bound that is not exact (its result is a union) passes its
// parts to a callback only when the callback's parameters have the bound's
// names and count: `|y| e` against `fn(x: i64) -> [i64, null]` keeps its
// result free, the check accepts, and the build refuses.
//
// command:  /home/eric/tmp/review-2026-11-16/bin/graphix --no-cache --check t-contains-a-fn-bound-parts-by-name.gx
//           /home/eric/tmp/review-2026-11-16/bin/graphix --no-cache t-contains-a-fn-bound-parts-by-name.gx
//           /home/eric/tmp/review-2026-11-16/bin/graphix-fuzz check t-contains-a-fn-bound-parts-by-name.gx
// expected: the check refuses `e <- "s"`, as it does with the callback
//           written `|x| e` ("e is '_N: unbound within [i64, null] and cannot
//           hold string") and with the exact bound `fn(x: i64) -> i64`.
// observed: --check exits 0; the run fails with "an instance at
//           fn(g: fn(y: _) -> string) -> i64 of a definition typed
//           fn<'a: fn(x: i64) -> [i64, null]>(..)"; graphix-fuzz prints
//           "DIVERGENCE — the check accepted what the build refused".
// The same holds for a callback with a labeled default the bound lacks:
//   let mk = |z| |#d = 0, x| z; let h = mk(e); .. f(h)
// and under a struct or a union in the bound
// (`'a: {k: fn(x: i64) -> [i64, null]}`, `'a: [fn(x: i64) -> [i64, null], null]`).
let e = never();
let f = 'a: fn(x: i64) -> [i64, null] |g: 'a| g(1)$ + 1;
let r = f(|y| e);
e <- "s";
r
